# no dev Case carries a tag, so routing degrades to all-failed and nothing is attributed per behavior

Evals  testdata/evalinspect/untagged.jsonl
  120 Cases — 100 dev, 20 held back
  0 distinct behaviors (tags)

  Everything below reads your tags as behaviors, because that is what routing
  does. If these tags name something else — priority, source, a date — the
  per-tag numbers and suggestions below do not apply to them. Kno cannot tell
  the difference.

No dev Case carries a behavior tag.

  ! flagged   ✓ ok   ? unknown   · reported, never flagged
  · 0% of dev Cases carry more than one behavior tag — a failure in those
    Cases testifies about every tag it carries, so per-behavior attribution is
    shared. (Heuristic: a tag is a label, not a claim about what a Case
    exercises. Reported, not flagged: there is no principled threshold for
    this.)
  ! no dev Case carries a tag, so routing runs in all-failed mode and
    attributes nothing per behavior
  ? behaviors_powered is unknown: no behavior tags to assess
  ! 100% of dev Cases carry no behavior tag
  ✓ the holdout has 20 Cases (20 is the minimum for a meaningful interval at
    validate)
  ? attribution_observed is unknown: no --value-run-id given

2 of 5 checks flagged.  If these tags are behaviors you would fix separately:
  - tag the 100 dev Cases that carry no behavior tag, or accept that routing
    will measure everything against everything
  - re-run with --value-run-id <id> to see which behaviors a run actually
    attributed

What each number claims: docs/what-the-numbers-mean.md
Designing evals: docs/evaluation-design.md

